 "questions": [
  {
   "id": 507015,
   "is_answer_order_important": false,
   "stimulus_html": "",
   "stem_html": "If the eighth term of an arithmetic sequence is 35 and the 40th term is 131, what is the constant difference and the first term of the sequence?",
   "answers": [],
   "hints": [],
   "formats": [
    "free-response"
   ],
   "combo_choices": [],
   "collaborator_solutions": [
    {
     "images": [],
     "solution_type": "summary",
     "content_html": "<p><span data-math=\"{a_8} = 35,{a_{40}} = 131\"></span></p>\n<p>When you know two terms, you can find the constant difference using this formula: <span data-math=\"d = \\frac{{{a_j} - {a_i}}}{{j - i}}\"></span>.</p>\n<p><span data-math=\"d = \\frac{{131 - 35}}{{40 - 8}} = \\frac{{96}}{{32}} = 3\"></span></p>\n<p>The first term in the sequence can be found using this formula: <span data-math=\"{a_1} = {a_i} - d\\left({i - 1} \\right)\"></span> and <span data-math=\"{a_8} = 35\"></span>.</p>\n<p><span data-math=\"{a_1} = 35 - 3\\left({8 - 1} \\right) = 35 - 3(7) = 35 - 21 = 14\"></span></p>\n<p>The constant difference is 3 and the first term is 14.</p>"
    },
    {
     "images": [],
     "solution_type": "detailed",
     "content_html": "<span data-math=\"d = 3\">d = 3</span>, <span data-math=\"{a_1} = 14\">{a_1} = 14</span>"
    }
   ],
   "community_solutions": []
  }
 ],
